task: handler-fusion-eff
agent: fable-fusion
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/handler-fusion-eff
worktree: ../okay-wt-eff
spec: specs/handler-fusion.md, "Stage B" (operator's go 2026-09-09, A then B)

why: stage 0 put two thirds of a pass's cost in the Free encoding
  re-materialising the program (every k(x) builds the next node);
  stage A removed the wrappers (-18% bytes, 7-11% time) and confirmed
  the rest is the tree. Eff is the Church encoding — the program is a
  function of its handler, no tree between them — and staged-effects.md
  measured 1.6-1.9x for exactly this shape on a bind chain.

what: a composite `!>` for a row (State % S + Writer % W first, then
  + Throws) assembled INLINE at compile time, the product accumulator
  carried in the answer type (S = Acc => (Acc, A)) so the handler is
  one static expression and the program runs once with no tree. Laws
  as stage 0: agreement with the nested Free runners for both orders,
  aborts included, on generated programs. JMH: the same 1000-op
  right-nested workload written as Eff vs Fused.stateWriter (13.8 us);
  bar 1.5x. Stated limit in the spec before the code: Eff is not
  stack-safe on left-nested binds — the for-comprehension road.

predict: 1.5-1.9x on the right-nested program, B/op down by most of the
  ~100 KB the tree costs; if it lands under 1.3x the mechanism to look
  at is Cont's own node allocation inside foldCont (the answer-type
  shifts), and the spec records it.
